+//! The card as a Wi-Fi station: the commands of Linux's `iwlmvm` for
+//! firmware API 77 (AX200 to AX211), in the order it sends them.
+//!
+//! After the firmware runs ([`Mvm::init`]): the platform's NVM data
+//! (AX210), the end of the firmware's start, the NVM's facts and the MAC
+//! address, the antennas, Bluetooth coexistence, the platform's latency,
+//! power (always awake), the region and the scan configuration; then the
+//! interface's MAC context. A scan is one UMAC scan request. Joining a
+//! network is a PHY context on its channel, the binding, the access
+//! point's station and its two queues (management and data) and session
+//! protection; once associated, the MAC context and the station say so and
+//! the firmware's rate control (TLC) gets the rates. Frames go as TX
+//! commands, with the CCMP/GCMP header this driver writes (the hardware
+//! encrypts); they come as RX MPDU packets, decrypted by the hardware with
+//! that header still in.
+//!
+//! The 9000 family's firmware (API 46) speaks older versions of these
+//! commands, with a calibrating program first and the host's own queues
+//! and rate control ([`gen1`]).
+//!
+//! Not yet: 40 MHz and wider (the station says 20 MHz HT, so access points
+//! send at 20 MHz), VHT and HE, block-ack agreements, power save, 6 GHz,
+//! TKIP and WEP, BIP (802.11w broadcast protection), restarting the
+//! firmware after an error.
